enerating such objects with ﬁxed number of nodes. Some details are given below. 17 2.6.1 Necklaces A necklace is an important combinatorial structure. Several schemes have been devel- oped for the generation of necklaces, Lyndon words and their variants. A simple and elegant algorithm is proposed in Fredricksen and Maiorana (Fredricksen and Maio- rana 1978) and Fredricksen and Kessler (Fredricksen and Kessler 1986) to generate the sets Pk (n) and Nk (n) in lexicographic order. This algorithm is known as FKM algo- rithm. The analysis of FKM algorithm is given in (Ruskey, Savage, and Wang 1992). Later Kevin et al. (Cattell, Ruskey, Sawada, Serra, and Miers 2000), give a recursive framework to generate prenecklaces, necklaces and Lyndon words. This framework is recursive version of FKM algorithm. The answer to the existence of Gray code for necklaces is given by (Ueda 2000). Recently, Vajnovszki answers the problem of generating unrestricted binary necklaces in Gray code order (Weston and Vajnovszki 2006). Later Vajnovszki and Weston give a generalized and optimized algorithm for arbitrarily large alphabet size (Vajnovszki 2007) (Vajnovszki 2008). 2.6.2 Necklace with Fixed Density The CAT algorithm to list necklaces with ﬁxed density of arbitrary base is given in (Ruskey and Sawada 1999).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nListing Words in Free Groups (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/317930286_Listing_Words_in_Free_Groups)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; In this paper, we analyze an algorithm due to Fredricksen, Kessler, and Maiorana (FKM), to show that necklaces can be generated in constant average time. ... We exhibit a correspondence between the binary cycles of length n and the lexicographic compositions of the integer n.\nA by-product product of our analysi...\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nFactorizing Words over an Ordered Alphabet.\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 1983\n\n  * Jean-Pierre Duval\n\nAn efficient algorithm to obtain a factorization of words over an ordered alphabet known as Lyndon factorization is presented. Applications of this algorithm are given to the computation of the least suffix of a word and the least circular shift of a word.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nAn algorithm for generating necklaces of beads in two colors\n\nArticle\n\n  * Sep 1986\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Harold Fredricksen\n  * Irving J. Kessler\n\nIn this paper we describe the necklaces of beads of length n in two colors and their equivalence to binary cycles from a circulating regiter of length n. We exhibit a correspondence between the binary cycles of length n and the lexicographic compositions of the integer n. We then give algorithms to generate the necklaces and the lexicographic compositions. We compare our algorithms to an exhaustive algoritm for generating the necklaces. We also give an algorithm for generating all necklaces of a specific density.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeletion and contraction in configuration spaces of graphs (https://www.academia.edu/91732174/Deletion_and_contraction_in_configuration_spaces_of_graphs)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.5 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; We also show that the generalized homology of unordered conﬁguration spaces is ﬁnitely generated as a representation of the opposite graph minor category. ... Describe the edge contraction homomorphism between graph braid groups, e.g. with respect to the Farley-Sabalka presentation of tree braid groups [FS05]. ... Conjecture 1.2.2 (Finite generation of LCS quotients).\nOur explicit space level construction of these maps provides a way to study the induced maps on π1 directly. Problem 1.2.1. Describe the edge contraction homomorphism between graph braid groups, e.g. with respect to the Farley-Sabalka presentation of tree braid groups [FS05]. DELETION AND CONTRACTION IN CONFIGURATION SPACES OF GRAPHS 3 F